{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments ["<b>Would that their heart were always like this, to fear Me.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): the expression 'would that' (מי יתן) is used because the Holy One, blessed be He, has already delivered dominion and free choice into the hand of man to do whatever he wishes, whether good or evil. And so the Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded: 'Everything is in the hands of Heaven except the fear of Heaven.'" "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: 'Would that their heart were always like this' — this is what the verse says (Psalms 14:7): 'מי יתן מציון ישועת ישראל.' King David, peace be upon him, said this verse twice in the book of Psalms. What is the reason? Rather, the master said 'מי יתן והיה לבבם זה,' and the disciple said (Numbers 11:29): 'ומי יתן כל עם ה׳ נביאים.' In this world neither the words of the master nor the words of the disciple were fulfilled, but in the world to come both are fulfilled. The words of the master — from where? (Ezekiel 36:26): 'ונתתי לכם לב בשר.' The words of the disciple — from where? (Joel 3:1): 'אשפוך את רוחי על כל בשר.'" "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: 'Would that their heart were always like this' — the Unique Name says: would that it ascend in pure thought, that they would fear Me all the days. And this teaches you that fear is not in His hand, and this is the meaning of 'Everything is in the hands of Heaven,' namely in the hand of the attribute called Heaven, 'except the fear of Heaven' — but it is above it. And what the Rabbis, of blessed memory, said, 'They should have said: You give it' — its explanation is that one should pray to that which is above this. And this is what the masters of truth, of blessed memory, said in tractate Ta'anit concerning R. Elazar ben Pedat, who was pressed for livelihood: the Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: 'Is it pleasing to you that I destroy the world? Perhaps you fell at the hour of livelihood.' Joined to this is their exposition: when Moses saw R. Akiva, whose flesh was being weighed and sold in the meat-markets, he said before Him: 'Master of the universe — this is Torah and this is its reward?!' He said to him: 'Be silent; thus it arose in thought.' And the explanation of this is that thus did Wisdom require in the selling of Joseph. Joined to this you will find in Bereshit Rabbah at its beginning, on the verse (Proverbs 8:30): 'ואהיה אצלו אמון' — the Torah said: 'I was the craft of the Holy One, blessed be He.' In the way of the world, when a king of flesh and blood builds a palace, he does not build it from his own knowledge but from the knowledge of the craftsman. And the craftsman does not build from his own knowledge, but he has blueprints (דפתריות) and ledgers (פנקסאות) to know how to make rooms and how to make alcoves and how to make upper chambers. Thus the Holy One, blessed be He, was looking into the Torah and building the world — end quote. And the explanation of this is that He likened the Shekhinah to a king of flesh and blood, and His great Name to the craftsman who fashions and makes the works, and Wisdom to the blueprints and ledgers, which are books in which letters are engraved. And therefore Moses, of whom it is written (Deuteronomy 33:21): 'וירא ראשית לו,' is called 'lawgiver' (מחוקק) — likewise in the language of the Talmud: 'a דפתרא of salted and unsalted fish, but not pepper.' And further, concerning Pharaoh: Pharaoh produced a דפתרא of divinity and began calling 'the god of Moab,' 'the god of Ammon,' 'the god of Sidon.' All of this is clear: that the king needs a craftsman, and the craftsman needs Wisdom, for the craftsman produces only according to what his intellect designs. If so, Wisdom is the beginning of all and through it all is completed. And this is the secret of the verse (Job 28:28): 'הן יראת אדני היא חכמה' — its explanation is that Wisdom, which is pure thought, is the fear of the Master of the world, blessed be He. And this is the secret of what is written (Isaiah 30:18): 'לכן יחכה ה׳ לחננכם ולכם ירום לרחמכם.' And you should be roused to ask about the expression 'יחכה' and the expression 'ירום.' And this is the secret of the verse that says (Psalms 143:1): 'באמונתך ענני בצדקתך' — and the faith here is Wisdom, from the verse that says (Habakkuk 2:4): 'וצדיק באמונתו יחיה,' and it is written (Ecclesiastes 7:12): 'החכמה תחיה בעליה.'" "<b>And I received</b> from the sage R. Yitzhak ben ha-Rav, of blessed memory, that King David, peace be upon him, mentioned this verse in the manner of an oath. And likewise the verse says (Psalms 89:50): 'נשבעת לדוד באמונתך.' And you should apply your mind to the language of the verse here."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {2 "The citation 'תהלים צג' is normalized to Psalms 14:7, matching the quoted phrase 'מי יתן מציון ישועת ישראל'; 'צג' likely reflects a corrupted or variant abbreviation. The Aramaic loan 'דפתריות' (blueprints/tablets) is retained and glossed on first occurrence; 'דפתרא' left transliterated where it appears in Talmudic and Pharaoh contexts."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:46:12.226146062Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}